AFKV2 host selftests
===================

Run ./run-afkv2-tests.sh from this directory, or use make run_tests in a
complete kernel source checkout. HOSTCC, CFLAGS and PYTHON select host tools
(standalone CC is also accepted).
AFKV2_SANITIZERS=1 additionally enables AddressSanitizer and UBSan.
AFKV2_SOURCE_ROOT can name the source checkout when running a copied test
package; the Makefile exports it automatically for separate output builds.

The codec tests include the production header directly and compare invented,
independently specified golden bytes. They cover truncation, category ranges,
length/padding/overflow, unaligned input, OOB descriptor ownership, response
correlation, token preservation and command ID packing.

The owned-copy test extracts the actual production function from afk.c, prints
its SHA256, and compiles it with synthetic command slots and CPU buffers. It
checks active/inactive/done slots, high device addresses, boundaries and copies,
and that only firmware 27 hands an unowned buffer to its retained-DART path.
Locks, DMA barriers and that path (afk_v27_access_remote()) are mocked: this
test does not qualify concurrency, DMA coherency, firmware, a graphics device
or any hardware display behavior.

All build products are temporary. No device node is opened and no GPU, firmware
or target machine is contacted. The extractor fails if production function
boundaries change, requiring review rather than silently testing a stale copy.

The inline EDID tests cover response context, status, declared/actual lengths,
EDID header/checksum/block limits, and continued rejection of inline responses
by the OOB matcher. The actual service-call body is extracted for short reply,
error status and timeout checks; its wire sender is mocked.

The DPTX HPD request is extracted with that service-call body and its timeout
wrapper. With legacy and AFK-v2 framing, a reply whose declared body stops
before the HPD echo, or runs past the bytes that arrived, is refused for both
HPD states; complete and padded replies are accepted and a wrong echo refused.

The real sender is also extracted to test a 128-byte mocked command payload
with a 32 KiB response capacity, OOB behavior, send failures and timeout
retention. The two production EDID readers are tested independently to
prove legacy checksum tolerance remains unchanged.

The complete encoded EDID request is 160 bytes including the queue entry,
consumes 192 bytes at a 64-byte stride, and needs a 256-byte empty ring. The
ring-admission block is extracted from the real transmitter to check this.
The late-publication notifier is extracted to test asynchronous hotplug
queueing and connector-registration, quiesce, routing and legacy guards.

The compact slot allocator is extracted to model DCP poweron cycles without
service teardown, on every compact endpoint (0x20-0x2f) and both DCPs, for
64 generations. A service re-announced after a poweron replaces the same
name, unit and binding in place, so the slot count stays bounded; the
hotplug endpoints 0x27-0x29 also replace within a generation. DPAVCTRL's
same-name, no-unit siblings announced in one generation stay separate, as
do different identities. Free-slot capacity, reader pins and pending
commands are checked
separately. Publication and teardown are mocked; this does not test firmware
power transitions or concurrent service users.
